  "query_preview": "..An active mid to late October weather pattern for our \nregion this week as our area is influenced by moist south to \nsouthwesterly flow. A shearing out trough currently resides within \nthis flow across Arizona with another disturbance tailing back into \nthe northern Gulf of California. We continue to see isolated to \nscattered showers with some embedded thunderstorms north of Tucson \nat this time with more development expected from Tucson south and \neastward over the next couple of hours. Visible satellite imagery \nshows an agitated cumulus field developing from Tucson south and \neast with SPC RAP mesoanalysis indicating around 500-1000 J/KG of \nMLCAPE. Given the adequate solar insolation today and convective \ntemperatures being reached, scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nexpected through the afternoon from Tucson eastward. A few of these \nthat produce more robust updrafts could be marginally severe for \nstrong wind gusts and hail. The SPC has placed locales from Tucson \neastward in a marginal risk for severe thunderstorms. Given the \nquick movement around 30 mph from these storms, rainfall amounts \nwill generally be 0.2\"-0.6\" within shower and thunderstorm activity, \nthough any localized locales that see multiple rounds/backbuilding \nof storms could receive in excess of 1 inch of rain. The WPC also \nhas our area in a marginal risk today for excessive rainfall. After \nsunset, shower and thunderstorm activity is expected to wane with \nthe loss of daytime heating, though can't rule out some isolated \nshower activity overnight mainly near the NM border.\n\nThe next weather system to impact our area will be an upper low and \nlongwave trough which will be located off the California coast \nTuesday. This system will move onshore Wednesday night into \nThursday, then northeast into the Great Basin Friday. With the \nabnormally moist southerly flow continuing Tuesday, isolated to \nwidely scattered showers with a slight chance of a thunderstorm \nmainly from Tucson north and eastward is expected. Coverage will be \nless than today given the lack of forcing and dynamics. \n\nThe most active period will be Wednesday into Thursday as our area \ngets more under the influence of the trough and its associated lift \nand dynamics. The chances of showers and thunderstorms (30 to 80 \npercent) ramps up Wednesday afternoon especially north of Tucson and \ncontinues through Wednesday night and Thursday with the exact end \ntiming dependent on how fast the trough axis moves east. It's early \nbut the convective parameters are certainly favorable for a few \nstronger thunderstorms as this system moves through, especially if \nthere are enough breaks in the cloud cover for greater instability. \nThe SPC has already placed our area in a Day 3 marginal risk \nWednesday. WPC has locales north of Tucson in a slight risk for \nexcessive rain on Thursday, with other locales in a marginal risk \nThursday. For Wednesday, most of southeast Arizona is in a marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall. QPF Wednesday through Thursday is \nexpected to be 0.25\" to 0.75\" for many locales with localized spots \nsuch as the mountains (upslope favored) and northern Graham/Greenlee \nCounties as high as 1 to 1.5 inches.\n\nDrier air moves in for Friday, though some lingering showers \npossible near the NM border, which could linger into the weekend as \nwell. \n\nTemperatures through Thursday will be at or slightly above normal, \nthen cooler than normal Friday into the upcoming weekend.",
